Rockchip DMA-BUF "system" heap: back it with contiguous CMA memory.

The RK356x NPU runs without an IOMMU ("rknpu iommu device-tree entry not
found!, using non-iommu mode"), so the buffers handed to it through
/dev/dma_heap/system must be physically contiguous.

The upstream system heap added by patch 320 allocates from a buddy page pool
(orders 1MB/64K/4K).  A buffer larger than the largest single order block is
therefore built from several independent blocks and the resulting sg_table
has many entries.  rknpu_mem.c derives the hardware base address from the
LAST scatterlist entry of the mapped sg_table:

	for_each_sgtable_sg(table, sgl, i) {
		phys = sg_dma_address(sgl);
		page = sg_page(sgl);
		length = sg_dma_len(sgl);
	}

so a scattered buffer makes the NPU program a mid-buffer address, it never
makes progress and the job fails:

	RKNPU: failed to wait job, task counter: 0
	RKNPU: job timeout, flags: 0x0:
	  core 0 irq status: 0x0, raw status: 0x0, require mask: 0x300,
	  task counter: 0x0

This patch keeps the exported heap name ("system", the name the RKNN runtime
opens) but replaces the page-pool allocation with a single physically
contiguous block taken from the default CMA area (cma_alloc()/cma_release()),
mirroring the vendor rk_cma_heap/rk_system_heap stack.  The block is described
by exactly one scatterlist entry, so the address rknpu_mem.c reads is the true
base address of a contiguous range and the NPU task counter advances.

The CMA area is the default one created from the "cma=128M" cmdline (or a DT
linux,cma node); no CMA size, cmdline or DTS change is needed.  CMA bounds
allocation granularity to page blocks, so small buffers may consume a full
alternate block, but RKNN model/tensor buffers are MB sized, which is the
intended use.

The page-pool/deferred-free libraries stay built (they are still selected by
the config) but are no longer used by this heap.

Only drivers/dma-buf/heaps/system_heap.c is changed.
